\description{
Fits a multinomial (baseline-category) logit model for a categorical
response by maximum likelihood or mean bias reduction, with the compiled
vector GLM engine. The model has one linear predictor for each non-reference
category, \verb{log\{Pr(Y = k) / Pr(Y = reference)\} = offset_k + x' beta_k}.
}
\details{
A \code{\link[=fastglm_source]{fastglm_source()}} is read one block at a time, so the
design is never held in memory as a whole. Each block (a data frame for a
formula source, or a list with \code{X}, \code{y}, and optional \code{weights} and
\code{offset}) supplies a factor, character, or count-matrix response. Offsets
are one column per non-reference category (\code{offset} names in
\code{\link[=fastglm_source]{fastglm_source()}}), or one column applied to every linear predictor. The
fit makes one preliminary pass for the category totals and the intercept
column, then the passes of the engine, then a fit of the intercept-only
model for \code{null.deviance}. \code{passes} counts the engine's passes for the
model, as for in-memory fits. Under the exact solver there is one pass at
the starting values, which also finds aliased columns, and one per step
length tried, so a fit without step halving makes \code{iter + 1} passes, and
bias reduction adds one pass per iteration and one at the start
(\verb{2 iter + 2}). Under the conjugate gradient solver, \code{passes} also counts
the information-vector products and the passes that confirm convergence,
so it exceeds \code{iter + 1}. \code{source.passes} counts
every pass over the source, including the preliminary pass and the
intercept-only fit. The fit keeps the source, not its rows: fitted
probabilities are not stored (\code{fitted.values}, \code{y}, \code{x}, \code{prior.weights},
and \code{offset} are \code{NULL}) and \code{residuals()} is not available. Use
\code{predict()} with \code{newdata}, or without it (optionally with a \code{sink} that
receives one block at a time) to reread the source. The covariance and
diagnostic functions also reread the source block by block, so it must
return the same rows in the same order.

Coefficients are ordered term-major: all \code{K - 1} coefficients of the
first design column, then those of the second, and so on, and are named
\code{"<level>:<term>"}. A design column that is a linear combination of earlier
columns is aliased: its coefficients are \code{NA} and the rank is reduced.

Every category must have a positive weighted number of observations,
because otherwise no finite maximum exists; an empty category stops the
fit with an error naming it. Rows with zero weight (or, for grouped data,
zero total count) contribute nothing and are not counted in \code{nobs}.
\code{df.residual} is \code{nobs * (K - 1)} minus the rank, and \code{null.deviance} is
the deviance of the intercept-only model with the same weights and
offsets.

For individual data the deviance is \code{-2} times the log likelihood. For
grouped (count-matrix) data the saturated log likelihood, the sum over
positive counts \code{y} of \code{w * y * log(y / m)} with \code{m} the row total, is
subtracted, so that \code{deviance} and \code{null.deviance} equal those of
\code{\link[stats:glm]{stats::glm()}} and \code{\link[=fastglm]{fastglm()}} for a grouped binomial response when
\code{K = 2}. \code{loglik}, \code{logLik()}, \code{AIC()}, and \code{BIC()} are not changed by this
convention. In a formula fit, unused levels of factor predictors (for
example after \code{subset}) are dropped, as in \code{\link[stats:glm]{stats::glm()}}, while the
response keeps all its levels.

Without bias reduction, a fit with separation has coefficients that
diverge. The iteration then stops by the usual rule, the fit warns that
fitted probabilities are numerically 0 or 1 (within 10 times the machine
epsilon, the threshold of \code{\link[stats:glm]{stats::glm.fit()}}), and \code{separation} is \code{TRUE}.
With \code{firth = TRUE} the flag is stored but no warning is given.
Bias-reduced fits near separation converge linearly rather than
quadratically, so their coefficients stop within a distance that shrinks
with \code{tol} (about 2e-4 at the default \code{tol = 1e-8} on a separated
example, and about 3e-6 at \code{tol = 1e-12}); lower \code{tol} for tighter
agreement with other software.
}
